4375, -0.0121612548828125, -0.01012420654296875, 0.013458251953125, -0.01212310791015625, -0.015411376953125, 0.03631591796875, 0.031982421875, -0.01177215576171875, 0.0110321044921875, -0.0024814605712890625, 0.007144927978515625, 0.03033447265625, -0.0262603759765625, -0.03240966796875, 0.00487518310546875, 0.00933074951171875, 0.00022268295288085938, -0.0140228271484375, 0.00799560546875, -0.018646240234375, -0.004024505615234375, 0.015228271484375, 0.0151519775390625, -0.01320648193359375, 0.022064208984375, -0.0174713134765625, -0.0033435821533203125, -0.022125244140625, 0.01715087890625, -0.00293731689453125, 0.031494140625, 0.042510986328125, 0.033782958984375, 0.0070648193359375, 0.0196075439453125, 0.059417724609375, -0.01131439208984375, 0.013519287109375, -0.027801513671875, 0.032684326171875, 0.01152801513671875, -0.029754638671875, 0.003986358642578125, -0.02392578125, 0.0191650390625, 0.0202789306640625, -0.0277862548828125, 0.0140838623046875, 0.0110015869140625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**1. Introduction and Related Work**\n\nDistrict heating networks are critical for decarbonizing urban heat supply, but their efficient operation is challenged by the integration of heterogeneous, time-varying renewable sources, the need for thermal storage, and increasingly complex, meshed network topologies. Existing optimization approaches\u2014including mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) [1], convex relaxations [2], and heuristic methods [3]\u2014struggle with the nonlinearities and combinatorial complexity inherent in these systems, especially for meshed networks, multiple time steps, and realistic operational constraints. Most prior work is limited to tree-like networks, linearized models, or small-scale instances, and does not address the full nonlinear, meshed, multi-supplier, and storage-coupled setting.\n\n**Limitations of Prior Work**\n\n- **MILP approaches [1]:** These methods linearize the network physics and are typically restricted to tree-like (acyclic) topologies. They cannot capture nonlinear heat losses or flow-temperature coupling, and do not scale to meshed networks due to the exponential growth in binary variables required for cycle handling.\n- **Convex relaxations [2]:** While these methods can handle some nonlinearities via convex approximations (e.g., piecewise-linearization of heat loss), they are limited to small or weakly meshed networks and often rely on simplifying assumptions that break down for realistic, cyclic topologies. The relaxations may not be tight, leading to suboptimal or infeasible solutions.\n- **Heuristic methods [3]:** Heuristics can handle larger and more complex networks, including meshed topologies and multiple suppliers or storage units, but they do not provide guarantees of global optimality. Their performance is highly problem-dependent, and they may fail to find feasible or high-quality solutions in challenging cases.\n- **Recent works [8,9]:** These address specific aspects such as improved heuristics or convex relaxations for small cycles, but do not provide a unified, globally optimal framework for the full nonlinear, meshed, multi-supplier, and storage-coupled problem.\n\n**Definitions of Table 1 Features:**\n- **Meshed networks:** Support for general network topologies with cycles (as opposed to tree-like, acyclic networks).\n- **Nonlinear heat loss:** Accurate modeling of temperature-dependent heat losses along pipes, as opposed to linear or approximate representations.\n- **Multiple suppliers/storage:** Ability to model networks with more than one heat supplier and with thermal storage units.\n- **Global optimality guarantees:** The method can certify that a solution is globally optimal for the original (nonlinear, nonconvex) problem.\n- **Explicit cycle elimination:** The algorithm includes constraints or procedures to ensure that flow variables respect the physical laws around cycles (e.g., Kirchhoff-type constraints), preventing unphysical solutions.\n- **Symmetry exploitation:** The method identifies and removes redundant solutions arising from network symmetries, reducing computational effort.\n- **Constraint reduction:** Techniques to eliminate redundant or inactive constraints, improving scalability.\n- **Primal heuristics:** Algorithms that quickly generate feasible solutions (not necessarily optimal) to provide upper bounds or warm starts for global optimization.\n\n**Summary Table of Methods**\n\n| Feature                        | [1] MILP | [2] Convex Relaxation | [3] Heuristic | This Work |\n|--------------------------------|----------|----------------------|---------------|-----------|\n| Meshed networks                | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n| Nonlinear heat loss            | \u2717        | \u2713 (approximate)      | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n| Multiple suppliers/storage     | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n| Global optimality guarantees   | \u2713        | \u2713 (relaxed)          | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Explicit cycle elimination     | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Symmetry exploitation          | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Constraint reduction           | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713         |\n| Primal heuristics              | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713         |\n\n*Legend: \"\u2713\" = full support; \"\u2713 (approximate)\" = partial or approximate support (e.g., via linearization); \"\u2717\" = not supported.*\n\n**Contributions**\n\nThis work addresses the above limitations and makes the following key contributions:\n- Proposes the first global optimization framework for stationary, nonlinear district heating networks with general (meshed) topologies, multiple suppliers, and thermal storage.\n- Accurately models nonlinear heat losses and flow-temperature coupling, without linearization or convex relaxation.\n- Introduces explicit cycle constraints to ensure physical feasibility in meshed networks.\n- Exploits network symmetries and reduces redundant constraints to improve computational efficiency.\n- Provides global optimality guarantees and incorporates primal heuristics for rapid feasible solution generation.\n\n**2. Precise Problem Statement**\n\nWe consider the stationary (single time step) optimization of a district heating network, subject to the following modeling assumptions:\n- **Perfect mixing:** At each mixing node, outgoing temperature is the flow-weighted average of incoming temperatures.\n- **Neglect of pressure:** Hydraulic (pressure) constraints are omitted; pressure is assumed to be managed by separate controllers.\n- **Single time step:** The model is static; dynamic and transient effects are neglected.\n- **No heat losses at nodes:** Heat losses occur only along pipes, not at nodes.\n\n**Sets and Definitions:**\n- \\( V \\): set of all nodes (junctions, consumers, suppliers, storage units, mixing nodes).\n- \\( E \\subseteq V \\times V \\): set of directed edges (pipes) in the network graph \\( G = (V, E) \\).\n- \\( S \\subseteq V \\): set of supplier nodes (heat injection).\n- \\( D \\subseteq V \\): set of demand (consumer) nodes (heat withdrawal).\n- \\( M \\subseteq V \\): set of mixing nodes (nodes with multiple incoming edges; perfect mixing assumed).\n- \\( V_{stor} \\subseteq V \\): set of storage nodes (nodes with thermal storage).\n- \\( \\mathcal{C} \\): set of fundamental cycles in \\( G \\), constructed via Paton's algorithm [10]. Each cycle \\( C \\in \\mathcal{C} \\) is an ordered sequence of edges forming a closed path.\n\n**Parameters:**\n- \\( d_i \\): net demand at node \\( i \\) (\\( >0 \\) for consumers, \\( <0 \\) for suppliers, \\( =0 \\) otherwise).\n- \\( C_i(x_i) \\): cost function for supplier \\( i \\).\n- \\( \\alpha_{ik} \\): heat loss coefficient for pipe \\( (i,k) \\).\n- \\( L_{ik} \\): length of pipe \\( (i,k) \\).\n- \\( c_p \\): specific heat capacity of water.\n- \\( T_{amb} \\): ambient temperature.\n- \\( T_{\\min}, T_{\\max} \\): minimum and maximum allowable temperatures.\n\n**Variables:**\n- \\( f_{ik} \\geq 0 \\): mass flow on edge \\( (i,k) \\).\n- \\( T_{ik}^{\\text{in}}, T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} \\): inlet and outlet temperatures on edge \\( (i,k) \\).\n- \\( x_i \\): heat supplied by supplier \\( i \\).\n- \\( Q_{ik} \\): heat delivered by pipe \\( (i,k) \\).\n- \\( s_j \\): storage state at storage node \\( j \\) (if applicable).\n\n**Optimization Problem:**\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\min_{f, T, x, s} \\quad & \\sum_{i \\in S} C_i(x_i) \\\\\n\\text{s.t.} \\quad\n& \\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji} - \\sum_{k: (i,k) \\in E} f_{ik} = d_i, \\quad \\forall i \\in V \\tag{1} \\\\\n& T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} = T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - \\alpha_{ik}(T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - T_{amb}) L_{ik}, \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\tag{2} \\\\\n& Q_{ik} = f_{ik} c_p (T_{ik}^{\\text{in}} - T_{ik}^{\\text{out}}), \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\tag{3} \\\\\n& T_{i}^{\\text{out}} = \\frac{\\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji} T_{ji}^{\\text{out}}}{\\sum_{j: (j,i) \\in E} f_{ji}}, \\quad \\forall i \\in M \\tag{4} \\\\\n& T_{ik}^{\\text{in}}, T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} \\in [T_{\\min}, T_{\\max}], \\quad f_{ik} \\geq 0, \\quad \\forall (i,k) \\in E \\tag{5} \\\\\n& x_i = \\sum_{k: (i,k) \\in E} Q_{ik}, \\quad \\forall i \\in S \\tag{6} \\\\\n& s_j = s_j^{\\text{in}} + \\sum_{k: (j,k) \\in E} Q_{jk} - \\sum_{i: (i,j) \\in E} Q_{ij}, \\quad \\forall j \\in V_{stor} \\tag{7} \\\\\n& \\sum_{(i,k) \\in C^+} f_{ik"}}